<p>I don’t remember the last desktop Macintosh I used as my full-time computer — probably whatever Mac was at my desk at the weekly newspaper I worked at in 1994. (A Macintosh Classic II, maybe? Sure. Let’s go with that.) When it came time to buy a computer of my own, I selected a PowerBook with my college graduation money, figuring my career path as a roving reporter would benefit from having a machine that could go with me. And it’s been a series of laptops ever since then, from iBooks to MacBook Pros to MacBook Airs.</p>
<p>The point is, after I opened the very large box Jason had dropped off at my front door and sat down to put <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/apple-clears-the-decks-ahead-of-its-biggest-announcement">Apple’s new M6 iMac</a> through its paces, it was the first time in a long time that I’ve used a desktop Mac for more than just a brief period. A big display, a beefy processor and all the ports I could ever want before having to plug into hubs and dongles — a fellow could get used to living like this.</p>
<p>That said, as much as I’ve enjoyed my time with the iMac, I’m not about to abandon the freedom of mobile computing in exchange for the power boost that comes from a more sedentary lifestyle. But for anyone more committed to desktops over laptops, there’s a lot to like about Apple’s latest iMac, especially if you’ve gone a few years in between upgrades.</p>
<p><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2024/11/m4-imac-review-gloriously-niche/">Apple’s previous iMac</a>, an M4-powered all-in-one, came out two years ago, and you’re not going to spot many differences between that version and the latest model. Even owners of <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2023/11/m3-imac-review-keep-playing-the-hits/">2023’s M3 iMac</a> aren’t likely to be moved by this latest edition.</p>
<p>But if you’re making do with an iMac powered by an M1 chip — or, heaven forfend, a model that predates the move to Apple Silicon — the M6 iMac promises to be a significant upgrade over what you have. And the M6 processor running the show is a big reason why.</p>
<h2>All about the M6</h2>
<p>If I had the space on my desk to accommodate both an M6 iMac and the M4 iMac it replaces in Apple’s lineup, I wouldn’t be able to spot any surface changes. Like its predecessor, the M6 iMac offers a 24-inch Retina display with 4.5K resolution. The display still promises 500 nits of brightness, and the 21.5-by-18.1-inch frame and 5.8-inch-deep stand go unchanged. Apple’s even offering the same seven color options as before, with my review unit coming in a fetching purple.</p>
<p>The big changes come under the hood in the form of that M6 processor, which offers <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/08/apple-unveils-m6-m5-ultra-chips-and-updates-its-desktop-macs/">a new mix of cores and two Neural Engines</a>, providing tangible boosts over the M4.</p>
<p>Like I said, though, M4 iMac owners are unlikely to be eyeballing the new machine with envy, especially since Apple upped the base price on the iMac by $200 to $1,499 back in June, and that price is maintained by the new M6 model.</p>
<h2>M6 iMac performance notes</h2>
<p>To that end, Apple’s promising a 5x improvement in AI workflows from the M4 iMac, courtesy of those Neural Engines. Obviously, anyone moving from an Intel-based Mac can expect a dramatic increase in performance, and early-generation Apple silicon Mac users will also see solid benefits. I don’t have any comparable all-in-ones lying around my house, but I do own an M3 MacBook Air equipped with 8GB of RAM, and the M6 iMac with 16GB of RAM that I’m testing smokes that laptop, as you might expect.</p>
<p>Specifically, when I ran Geekbench 7 on both machines, the M6 iMac posted single and multicore scores of 3,967 and 21,078, respectively. Those results beat the comparable numbers posted by my MacBook Air by 39% and 87%. Likewise, the M6 iMac’s GPU score was just about 4x better than what the laptop could put out.</p>
<p>To put that in real-world terms, the M6 chip powering the latest iMac is perfectly capable of handling whatever you want to throw at it. You can also expect smooth, detailed graphics when you&#8217;re playing games.</p>
<p>That processing muscle also comes into play when you’re handling tasks like editing photos, which was a breeze for me on the M6 iMac. Also helping matters is that display, which really calls out the subtle nuances of an image better than smaller screens, even my MacBook Air, as good as its 13-inch Liquid Retina display may be on its own.</p>
<p>After all, the entire idea behind the iMac is that it can satisfy people who want a desktop but don’t necessarily need the power (and price tag) that comes with a <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2025/03/m4-max-mac-studio-review-a-familiar-face/">Mac Studio</a>. That could include creative pros whose hardware budget tops out at around $2,000 as well as families who need a dependable machine that multiple people can turn to.</p>
<h2>The other iMac changes</h2>
<p>The M6 is easily the most substantive change Apple made to this version of the iMac, but it’s not the only under-the-hood improvement. The M6 iMac also features Apple’s N1 chip, which supports Wi-Fi 7 and Bluetooth 6. The M4 iMac’s wireless specs topped out at Wi-Fi 6E and Bluetooth 5.3.</p>
<p>Like Wi-Fi 6E, Wi-Fi 7 supports 6GHz, 5GHz and 2.4GHz bands, but with greater bandwidth and wider channels. It’s also capable of delivering faster wireless speeds, while support for Multi-Link Operation means your iMac can send and receive data across different bands. The end result is better, more efficient throughput.</p>
<p>Of course, for your M6 iMac to reap the full benefits of Wi-Fi 7, you’re going to need to connect to a Wi-Fi 7 router. Maybe you’ve recently upgraded your networking equipment to support the latest standards, maybe you haven’t. But even if it’s the latter, the M6 iMac’s support for the latest wireless connectivity standards means you’ve future-proofed your computer for your next purchase of a router or access point.</p>
<h2>Configuring your M6 iMac</h2>
<p>As noted above, the M6 iMac starts at $1,499. That gets you a machine with a 10-core CPU and GPU chip along with two rear Thunderbolt ports, 16GB of memory and 256GB of storage.</p>
<p>That’s not the machine I tested. Instead, my review unit came with the 12-core M6 chip and four Thunderbolt ports. That also means a 2.5Gb Ethernet port is included on my iMac; for the two-port models, it’s a $30 add-on. My machine came configured with the standard 16GB of RAM, but storage space was upgraded to 512GB. As configured, that machine would set you back $1,899. If you maxed out the memory and storage while also adding nano-texture glass to reduce glare and reflections on the display, you could pay as much as $3,299 for your all-in-one Mac.</p>
<p>As for that nano-texture option, I really didn’t miss it on my iMac, but I had the luxury of testing the machine in a basement office without much in the way of harsh lighting, either from the overhead lights or the window in the room facing away from the Mac. If you set up your computer in less forgiving settings — a cubicle, say, or a desk next to a window — that $200 nano-texture add-on (only available on the higher-end configurations) might save you a lot of squinting and a little bit of buyer’s remorse.</p>
<h2>Making sense of the iMac</h2>
<p>The M6 iMac doesn’t reinvent Apple’s all-in-one offering so much as it restates things, adding a more advanced processor, improved wireless connectivity and not much else. That’s not a criticism — just an honest assessment of who should consider making this particular machine their next Mac.</p>
<p>If it’s been more than three or four years since you’ve last upgraded your desktop, the M6 iMac offers enough horsepower to justify getting a new machine. And if this would be your first Mac all-in-one after trying out other computing options — a long line of laptops, say — this is a solid iteration for the iMac lineup.</p>
